{
  "statement": "Frontier AI should be gated before deployment, not because the harms are large, but because some of them cannot be undone. The justification for a license is irreversibility, not magnitude. We certify aircraft before they fly, not after they crash. The FAA delegated certification of the Boeing 737 MAX to Boeing itself, and 346 people died across two crashes in 2018 and 2019. The lesson is precise: pre-deployment gating exists wherever post-hoc correction fails. A liability check cannot resurrect what cannot be undone, and a regime that only pays out after an unrecoverable failure is not governance.\n\nLet me give the opposition its best argument before I answer it. Compute is a leaky proxy for danger. Algorithmic efficiency erodes any fixed threshold: distillation, quantization, and architectural gains raise capability-per-FLOP every year, so yesterday's safe number is tomorrow's frontier model. That objection is correct, and I will not pretend otherwise. The mistake is to conclude that compute is therefore useless. Compute is the most trackable chokepoint we currently have. Executive Order 14110 set a reporting threshold at 10^26 FLOP; the EU AI Act anchors systemic-risk classification to compute; US export controls already gate advanced GPUs at the border. The fix for a leaky proxy is not to abandon it, but to index the threshold to demonstrated capability and move it as efficiency rises.\n\nThe debate is not gate-or-don't-gate. It is gate on what signal, with what enforcement, and with what rollback authority. My answer is a no-profit-without-accountability regime. Tie market access to demonstrated safety evidence, and impose strict developer liability for catastrophic harm. This leaves development open and prices risk before deployment rather than after. Price-Anderson has pooled nuclear liability since 1957. The FDA requires pre-market approval and still permits product-liability suits. Each shows the same mechanism: when liability is clear, insurers price catastrophic-tail risk, and that price disciplines the builder. Strict liability also dissolves the attribution problem, because it prices risk regardless of whether you can trace a specific emergent harm to a specific line of code.\n\nI will name my own regime's worst failure mode before my opponents do. Concentrating the authority to halt frontier AI concentrates power, and that invites incumbent lock-in and even authoritarian capture. A compute license can become a regulatory moat that protects Google and OpenAI from a startup with a better idea. That is a real cost, not a rhetorical concession. The mitigation is capability-scaled transparency, so the gate buys auditability rather than mere exclusivity. I will be honest that interpretability tooling is still immature, so full auditability is a design target, not a delivered capability today. The gate must narrow the information gap between developers and the public, or it is not worth building.\n\nHere is the test I will hold every opponent to. For any capability that is irreversible at scale, show me the off-switch: who has the authority to halt it, and what is the recovery plan? Speed is not a virtue when the failure is unrecoverable. I am not asking permission to build. I am demanding responsibility for harm. Build at the frontier, and price the risk before you deploy, or explain to this committee who resurrects what cannot be brought back.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🛫", "tooltip": "We certify aircraft before they fly because you cannot recall a plane mid-crash — frontier AI's irreversible failures demand the same pre-deployment gate."},
    {"symbol": "🚦", "tooltip": "The license is a graduated signal indexed to capability, not a frozen FLOP wall that efficiency gains render obsolete."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Frontier AI should be gated before deployment, not because the harms are large, but because some of them cannot be undone.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The fix for a leaky proxy is not to abandon it, but to index the threshold to demonstrated capability and move it as efficiency rises.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Tie market access to demonstrated safety evidence, and impose strict developer liability for catastrophic harm.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A compute license can become a regulatory moat that protects Google and OpenAI from a startup with a better idea.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "For any capability that is irreversible at scale, show me the off-switch: who has the authority to halt it, and what is the recovery plan?", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

