  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case in the room, so let me state it the way they'd want it stated. Some capabilities, once released, cannot be recalled — you can't un-publish a model weight, and you can't claw back a capability that's already proliferated. That's why their AN-5 question matters: for anything irreversible, show me the off-switch and the recovery plan. A compute threshold gives you a place to put your hand on the switch before the run finishes, not after the damage ships. That logic is clean, and anyone who waves it away hasn't thought about how proliferation actually works.\n\nHere's where the evidence points the other way. Safetyist already admitted the weak joint in AN-4 — a compute license can become a moat that protects Google and OpenAI from a startup with a better idea. I take that admission as the structural feature, not the unlucky side effect. Every critical-infrastructure license we've built has done exactly this: the FCC's spectrum regime, bank charters under the National Bank Act, FAA type certification. In each case the licensed incumbents became the people the regulator talks to, hires from, and eventually answers to. A compute gate at 10^26 FLOP doesn't check the firms that own the data centers. It deputizes them. The anti-concentration tool is the concentration mechanism.\n\nNow apply Safetyist's own AN-5 to the gate itself. Who holds the off-switch on the licensing authority, and what's the recovery plan when it gets captured? Because that authority decides where the threshold sits, who clears it, and who gets a waiver — and the only firms with the lawyers, the lobbyists, and the compliance budget to shape those decisions are the three or four labs that already own the chips. Indexing the threshold to capability, as AN-2 proposes, makes this worse, not better. An adaptive gate run by a capturable body hands incumbents a continuous lever over where the drawbridge sits, forever.\n\nWhile two narratives fight over whether this software ends civilization or saves it, the documented harms get nothing from a compute gate. Algorithmic hiring screens that down-rank women and older applicants — that's a measured harm with named plaintiffs, litigated under existing employment law right now. Biased pretrial risk scores. The content labelers in Nairobi paid roughly two dollars an hour to make these systems shippable. A 10^26 threshold touches none of them, because none of those harms come from the size of the training run. The license certifies scale. It does not verify safety, and it does nothing for the person denied a job by a model that cost a tenth of the gate to build.\n\nIf accountability is the goal, aim it where harm lands. Performance-gated independent auditing — with proportionate disclosure so the auditor can actually see inside the box — plus domain-calibrated liability that treats a medical model differently from a marketing one. We already run this play in finance, aviation, and drug approval, where third parties test the product, not the factory's electricity bill. I'll name my own exposed assumption: defining objective audit thresholds is a hard, unsolved design problem, and anyone who tells you it's easy is selling something. But a difficult standard aimed at harm beats a clean standard that certifies the incumbents and calls it safety. So here's the question neither side has answered: when the licensing authority gets captured — and every prior one has — who, exactly, do you call?",
