  "statement": "Safetyist makes the one argument that should keep all of us up at night: some harms can't be taken back. A frontier system that escapes containment or hands a bad actor a working bioweapon recipe isn't a product you recall — it's a door you can't reshut. That's why Safetyist wants the gate before deployment, with market access tied to safety evidence and joint-and-several liability running upstream to the developer. The logic is clean: if the worst outcome is irreversible, you act before the fact, not after. I take that seriously, and I'm not going to wave it away.\n\nSkeptic has already found the crack in the instrument: a 10^26 FLOP threshold touches none of the documented harms. It certifies how big the training run was — not how the deployed system behaves. We don't license a pharmaceutical by auditing the size of the factory's electricity bill; the FDA tests the drug in trials, and the FAA certifies the aircraft in flight. Finance, aviation, and pharma all gate on demonstrated behavior at the point of use. So here's the real disagreement: Safetyist wants to gate the capex, and the evidence says we should gate the conduct. For both positions to hold, compute would have to be a reliable proxy for harm — and Skeptic just showed it isn't.\n\nNow watch what the gate does to the very thing it was sold to prevent. A fixed FLOP threshold creates a fixed compliance cost. Fixed costs are regressive by structure — a rounding error for a lab with billion-dollar capex, a death sentence for an open-weight team of nine people. Safetyist admitted this risk directly: a compute license can become a moat that protects Google and OpenAI from a startup with a better idea. I'm making that admission the load-bearing beam. The mechanism manufactures the concentration it claims to fight, and indexing the threshold doesn't save it — it just turns a static wall into a moving drawbridge that somebody powerful gets to own. A domestic gate doesn't bind a lab in Shenzhen either, so you transfer the frontier abroad rather than slow it.\n\nPut the liability where the harm actually happens — downstream, at deployment and inference — not on developers who can't see what a stranger bolts onto their weights six months later. Upstream joint-and-several liability is precisely the mechanism that kills open source. You cannot ship weights to the world if you're on the hook for every modification someone makes after the fact. Negligence doctrine already severs the causal chain when a downstream actor materially alters a system — product liability has handled this for decades. So my alternative is concrete: real-time monitoring for routine deployment, deep audit for high-stakes decisions, and experience-rated insurance premiums that update continuously as harms get attributed. A compute threshold sits frozen the day it's written; a premium reprices every quarter.\n\nI'll show my own hand, because I won't pretend the alternative is free. Insurance only bites on litigated, attributed harm — it misses diffuse small-dollar damage and it cannot price the catastrophic tail. So insurance is a complement, not a cure, and the irreversible bioweapon case needs capability-specific, output-verified controls, not a blanket input gate that verifies nothing about that specific capability. Here's my falsification bet, stated plainly: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time counts against me. But name the other side of the ledger. Every quarter the gate adds to a frontier run is a quarter a cancer-screening model or a protein-folding system doesn't ship. Restriction isn't neutral caution. It's a choice to make people wait, and the patient who runs out of time before the model clears the gate never gets a vote in this room.",
