n rollback: a statutory sunset that forces re-authorization on a fixed clock, halt authority distributed across more than one body so no single captured office can hold the drawbridge, and transparency obligations that scale with the capability being gated so the public can see when the threshold is being gamed to protect incumbents. A gate with no recovery plan fails my own test — so here is the recovery plan. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people precisely because the FAA delegated certification to the manufacturer and built no independent halt authority into the loop; the lesson is not 'don't certify,' it is 'never let the certifier be captured by the certified.'\n\nThe charge that upstream developer liability destroys open source attacks a regime I did not propose. Blanket joint-and-several liability for every downstream modification would indeed make shipping weights suicidal — and that is exactly why my proposal is a compliant-release safe harbor instead. A developer who embeds provenance metadata and completes independent pre-release evaluation earns protection from strict liability for third-party misuse. Compliant open-weight releases are shielded, not killed; the liability lands on the actor who removes the provenance or skips the evaluation, not on the lab that did the work and published honestly. Index the evaluation threshold to capability and sub-frontier open releases face no gate at all — which keeps the regulatory moat off the small developer, the failure mode I named myself and still own.\n\nThe disagreement has quietly collapsed to one point. The accelerationist case already concedes that the irreversible bioweapon scenario needs capability-specific, output-verified controls — but output-verified controls applied before release are a pre-deployment evaluation gate. We are no longer arguing about whether to gate the catastrophic tail; we are arguing about what signal pulls the model into the gate. So name it: what is your pre-deployment verification mechanism for the irreversible case, and who has the authority to stop the release if it fails? And to the separate worry that this whole regime ignores biased hiring screens and pretrial risk scores — it does not, because those harms ride a different instrument. Uniform disclosure and incident-reporting standards reach the diffuse documented damage that no compute gate ever touches. The tripwire guards the tail; the reporting standard guards the everyday. They are different layers, and a regime that builds both is not diverting from present harm — it is refusing to choose between the harm it can see today and the harm it cannot undo tomorrow.",
