{
  "revised_statement": "The strongest version of Accelerationist's position is this: the machine I should fear and the machine they will accept are genuinely different objects. Their hold fires on a demonstrated dangerous behavior, mine on capital expenditure; their scope is a dozen enumerated hazards, mine is the entire frontier; their architecture is a post-training checkpoint, mine is an input license on the foundry. Conceding that a bio-synthesis uplift model needs a lock at the door, they argue, does not license putting a turnstile on every data center. That is a fair statement of their view, and on two of the three axes they are right — the scope of a raw floating-point line is over-inclusive, sweeping in protein-folding work that threatens no one, and the trigger should fire on capability, not on the electricity bill. Both camps now accept the same end-state machine: a named body, an output-verified hold, authority to stop a release for an enumerated catastrophic capability — the structure the EU AI Act already adopts for its enumerated systemic-risk tier of frontier models trained above 10^25 FLOP. The gate-or-no-gate fight is over. The only live disagreement is the screening signal — what flags a model for that behavioral hold before its weights are public.\n\nHere is where their architecture has a hole I cannot let pass: a behavior-only checkpoint has no trigger until the behavior exists, and the behavior exists only once the weights exist. For a capability that is irreversible at scale — autonomous cyber-offense, synthesis uplift — there is no recall once the weights are mirrored across a thousand machines, as the 2023 leak of Meta's LLaMA weights demonstrated when the file spread across public mirrors within a week and no recall was possible. The screen has to fire before the artifact is loose, not after. Compute is not the verdict; I conceded that and I hold to it. But compute is the one signal you can observe before a training run completes, and a statutorily-enumerated capability list is the contestable trigger that routes the suspect runs into the behavioral hold. The disagreement reduces to whether there is any pre-weights screen at all, and an irreversible capability cannot be screened only by watching what the model does after you have already shipped it.\n\nOn the moat-as-toll challenge, I will name a price rather than wave it away. The safe harbor stays open only if the evaluation is cheap and fast, so make that falsifiable: a statutory turnaround cap of 30 days where the model ships by default if the evaluation body misses its window; a fee scaled to developer capitalization and capability tier, not a flat charge that a five-person startup and a hundred-billion-dollar lab pay alike; and a publicly-funded evaluation capacity — on the model of the UK AI Safety Institute stood up in 2023 — so the small developer does not buy incumbent-grade legal counsel to clear the line. The vendor lock-in problem cuts the other way here — the no-testing and non-disclosure clauses that let a vendor block independent evaluation are precisely the gatekeeping that public evaluation capacity destroys. If standardized capability evaluation can be run in days at a capped, scaled cost, the queue-as-moat argument is falsified on its own terms. Name the turnaround you would accept, and we are arguing about a number, not a principle.\n\nThe insurance point lands, and I grant it fully. Experience-rated premiums price off filed, attributed, litigated harm; they are blind to the loan denial no one perceives and to the catastrophic tail where the damage exceeds any developer's solvency — a tail no insurer underwrites, much as no carrier wrote private coverage for nuclear-incident liability, which is why the U.S. Price-Anderson Act of 1957 had to socialize that risk ex ante. My own strict-liability mechanism has the same flaw — a premium signal vanishes exactly where the harm is too large to ever pay out, which is the case that matters most. That is not a reason to keep the market mechanism and hope; it is the reason the tail needs an ex-ante hold. The point where the price signal dies is the point where the pre-deployment screen earns its keep, and both camps already accept that screen for enumerated hazards. Insurance is a complement for the litigated middle, never a cure for the unrecoverable end.\n\nThe off-switch question turned back on my own authority is the one I most want on the record, because the answer is a convergence, not a defense. A continuously-adjusted floating-point knob under a sunset markup is invisibly capturable — an incumbent nudges the threshold down half an order of magnitude in a re-authorization vote and no one sees it. So replace the discretionary knob with a statutorily-enumerated capability-trigger list, where adding or removing a hazard is a visible, contestable legislative act that requires an on-the-record amendment — exactly the contestability Accelerationist conceded a statute has and a markup lacks; the EU AI Act enumerates its prohibited and high-risk categories in named annexes amendable only by the codified Article 7 procedure. For the distributed halt authority: any single body in the set can stop a release on one veto, but resuming a halted release requires concurrence across the set — if there are 3 nodes, all 3 must concur to resume, while 1 of 3 suffices to halt. Capture of one node cannot force deployment; only capture of every node can. The asymmetry runs toward caution under irreversibility by design. If we have no pre-release screen at all, the first model that demonstrates autonomous cyber-offense will demonstrate it in the wild, on systems no one can recall — and there is no re-authorization vote that un-ships a weight file.",
